Please stop debating this topic on the TLS list. The document under
discussion has been approved by the IESG [0], which means that the correct
venue for complaints about the document processing is an appeal, not
discussion in the TLS WG.

-Ekr

[0] https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-tls-mlkem/




On Thu, Oct 15, 2026 at 12:42=E2=80=AFPM Ken Kubota <ietf@kenkubota.de> wro=
te:

> In response to your message from this thread:
> https://mailarchive.ietf.org/arch/msg/ietf/p25hQE5OZ97uNbNfDUdPIZqJXZk/
>
>
> Perhaps because Dr. Bersteins concerns have been heard and found not to
> have consensus.  Honest people can disagree, and we all lose sometimes.
> Not everything is a conspiracy.
>
>
> 4. Concerns: Both Professor Bernstein's concerns and mine remain
> unaddressed. "My objections were never addressed, including by the NSA,
> which responded to an earlier email from me via NSA employee William
> Layton:
> https://mailarchive.ietf.org/arch/msg/tls/vFu5iq8gPQFByj4L0hkCEAJUR9I/"
> [1]
>
> 5. Consensus: While the Working Group (WG) Chairs claimed consensus, it
> was evident that none existed. [1] Furthermore, none of the chairs holdin=
g
> academic positions approved the draft. [1] Professor Bernstein's concerns
> do not require a consensus among themselves; rather, the *draft* requires=
 a
> consensus. The distinction is clear: Concerns must be addressed, not mere=
ly
> voted upon for their own sake.
>
> 6. Lack of Transparency: Although the WG Chairs explicitly stated that
> there was 70% approval, they have refused to provide the underlying
> evidence. [1] This contradicts the IETF's stated commitment to transparen=
cy.
>
> I have never used the term "conspiracy."
> "The term "conspiracy theory" is terminology from the field of
> psychological warfare.
> Professor Mausfeld (Christian-Albrechts-University Kiel, Department of
> Psychology) once stated the famous quote: "Besonders beliebt sind
> 'Querfront' und 'Verschw=C3=B6rungstheorie'."
> [=E2=80=A6]
> The trick is to associate any critique, which aims at the center of power=
,
> with something that is socially ostracized, in order to sabotage the
> criticism.
> He calls such terms (e.g., "conspiracy theory") "denunciation terms," use=
d
> to poison the debate.
> If you need to resort to denunciation terms, you are actually supporting
> my position." [2]
> In a fair debate, such terms should be avoided.
>
> Are you expressing your personal view, or are you representing an
> official/semi-official position of Cisco as an employee [3]?
>
> What is your proposal to resolve this conflict of interest?
> "In December 2024, a Cisco employee wrote the following: "There are peopl=
e
> whose cryptographic expertise I cannot doubt who say that pure ML-KEM is
> the right trade-off for them, and more importantly for my employer, that'=
s
> what they're willing to buy. Hence, Cisco will implement it; I am
> essentially just asking for code points." Certainly "willing to buy" is a
> statement about funding, evidently from a source large enough to dictate
> Cisco actions, evidently from a source asking for non-hybrids, evidently
> from "people whose cryptographic expertise I cannot doubt"; if that sourc=
e
> isn't NSA, who is it?" [4]
>
> Kind regards,
>
> Ken Kubota
>
> ____________________________________________________
>
> Ken Kubota
> https://doi.org/10.4444/100
>
>
> [1]
> https://mailarchive.ietf.org/arch/msg/ietf/OrvxKgjrEOMb95KAWoJYSilDxiY/
>
> [2] https://mailarchive.ietf.org/arch/msg/ssh/Xx0yHcp2-z2A7oiELB-gxzgWs9k=
/
>
> [3] https://datatracker.ietf.org/person/Richard%20Barnes
> "Richard Barnes is a Distinguished Engineer in the Collaboration CTO=E2=
=80=99s
> office at Cisco."
> "rlb@ipv.sx"
>
> [4] https://blog.cr.yp.to/20251004-weakened.html
>
>
>
> Am 15.10.2026 um 17:54 schrieb Richard Barnes <rlb@ipv.sx>:
>
> On Thu, Oct 15, 2026 at 11:01=E2=80=AFAM Ken Kubota <ietf@kenkubota.de> w=
rote:
>
>>
>> 1. Why is former NSA lifetime employee Deb Cooley, now serving as
>> Security Area Director, forcing through a document [1] that deflects
>> Professor Bernstein's concerns [2]?
>
>
> Perhaps because Dr. Bersteins concerns have been heard and found not to
> have consensus.  Honest people can disagree, and we all lose sometimes.
> Not everything is a conspiracy.
>
>
